e  over double this figure you start to see  just how behind venezuela really is so  why is venezuela so poor despite having  the world's largest oil reserves  taking a look back it's definitely not a  lack of crying that has screwed over  venezuela in fact venezuela has been  trying to capitalize on their oil  reserves for over a hundred years it all  started in the year 1908 when a man  named juan vicente gomez became the  president of venezuela gomez was an  ambitious man who wanted to slash  venezuelan dead and revolutionized the  country and the way he sought to  accomplish this was by leveraging the  country's oil reserves at the time  people knew that venezuela had enough  oil for commercial purposes but no one  could have imagined the full magnitude  of just how much was actually available  president gomez however was willing to  roll the dice and test the country's  luck so he handed out oil concessions to  his closest friends and told them to go  enrich themselves and the country these  guys would turn around and hire oil  companies to come in and begin to hunt  within the next several years these  companies would find several viable oil  reserves however the blowout discovery  didn't come to 1922 when shell struck  oil in the maracaibo basin this well was  able to produce over a hundred thousand  barrels of oil per day by itself as you  would guess it didn't take long for more  oil companies to flood in it and they  were able to pump up annual production  to 137 million barrels of oil by the end  of the 1920s for perspective saudi  arabia didn't find oil till 1938 so  venezuela definitely had the first  mover's advantage but what they didn't  have was good management you see when  you find oil it's not as simple as just  cashing in the checks and living the  high life you have to reinvest the  profits and diversify the economy if you  want the money to actually create a  long-term change president gomez however  didn't do either of these now this isn't  to say that he spent the money in four  ways per se  of course he enriched himself and his  friends but he also spent significant  amounts on public projects and slashing  the country's debt this might seem like  a good use of money but it actually  leads to a phenomenon called dutch  disease dutch disease is when a country  experiences a massive influx of economic  growth within one sector which diverts  the entire country's focus  everyone suddenly wants to work in this  one industry or open a business in this  industry and while it's definitely a  good idea to follow opportunity this  sort of behavior leads to the rest of  the economy to fall to the wayside and  become underdeveloped in relation in  other words the country becomes overly  dependent on just one part of the  economy and this is exactly what  happened in venezuela given that oil  accounted for 90 of their exports by the  time gomez died this situation has only  gotten worse over the years as the oil  accounts for 95 of venezuela's exports  today but at the time the venezuelans  didn't realize what was ahead of them  and they would simply lean further into  their oil dependence  now that venezuela had proven their  viability to the western world it was  them who had the leverage you see in the  beginning it was the other way around  all these oil companies had to come in  and make a massive gamble on venezuela  but now that that gamble had proven  fruitful venezuela could start making  demands one of the first demands the  country made was for a larger proportion  of the oil revenue to flow into the  government in 1943 the government  introduced the hydrocarbons law which  required that foreign companies hand  over half of their oil profits to the  state this proved to be extremely  lucrative as venezuela's income would  grow six-fold in the following five  years but venezuela was just getting  started over the next few decades  venezuela would slowly nationalize the  entire oil industry in the meantime they  were also pushing for political reform  and trying to escape a history of  dictators one piece of this puzzle was  the punto fijo pact in 1958 this  agreement stipulated that the oil money  would be split between the three main  political parties in venezuela and the  respective proportions would be  determined by the popular vote at first  glance this sounds like a decent system  by splitting up the oil money you  ensured that one political party  couldn't just overpower the others but  at the same time it is also opened up  the country to loads of embezzlement i  mean who was going to make sure that  these political parties would actually  use the money in effective methods the  answer is no one while this storm was  starting to brew inside venezuela to the  rest of the world it seemed like  venezuela was killing it in 1960 they  would join up with the middle eastern  countries to found opec this was  essentially an agreement that these  countries would work together to  maximize all profits for all member  countries and now that venezuela had the  middle east on their side they were able  to become even more aggressive with  their own oil endeavors that same year  venezuela established a state-owned oil  company and increased taxes on oil  companies to 65  at this point it was just a matter of  time until venezuela nationalized oil  production all it would take is one more  monumental event and that's exactly what  would happen in the 1970s with the oil  embargo in 1973 opec would implement a  five-month embargo on countries that  supported israel in the yom kippur war  and one of these countries was the  united states as all of america's demand  shifted to non-opec countries oil prices  would quadrupola which was extremely  lucrative for all oil producing  countries but especially venezuela  venezuela would end up boasting the  highest per capita income in latin  america and this is exactly the push  they needed to completely nationalize  the country's 