 we should note that  chavez didn't really do anything to fix  the corruption problem if anything he  partook in the same activities as he  would eventually grow to be a  billionaire while chavez was growing his  world venezuela was quickly losing  theirs take the 2002 venezuelan awe  strike for example tens of thousands of  oil workers would go on strike and  demand for a new election to replace  chavez instead of addressing their  concerns though chavez would just go  ahead and fire 18 000 oil workers while  chavez was definitely able to show who  was in charge this would take a massive  toll on the country's oil production  venezuela was not only becoming more  dependent on oil revenue but our  production was declining this was simply  a disaster waiting to happen but chavez  would actually never see the  consequences as he would die due to  health problems in march of 2013  less than a year later oil prices would  crash because saudi arabia would decide  to start a price war the entire world  would feel the pain of their actions but  most of them would survive to see the  other side the same however could not be  said about venezuela  given that venezuela was basically 100  dependent on oil when this crash and  prices took place the country's economy  basically fell off a cliff the  government would try to stimulate the  economy by printing money and injecting  it into the economy but the problem was  that there was nothing to stimulate  venezuela had no economy it was just oil  and that's it so when oil went belly up  the country went the belly up and it was  no time before hyperinflation settled in  and that brings us to where we are today  looking back it's not like venezuela was  not able to leverage their resources or  pump up production or pulling loads of  money the problem is that none of this  money actually went to anything useful  virtually all of it went to corruption  and social programs when it should have  gone to developing the economy over time 